The page has three separate notebook entries.

(1) End of the calculus-of-variations entry begun on the previous page. Earlier Hilbert proves an auxiliary lemma. If f is uniformly approximated by rational functions R_n, and F(f)=0 forces the value 0 at x=0, then the R_n can be modified so that each is divisible by x^2. Hence f is divisible by x^2.
Here he states the programme. Let y(x) minimize J[y] = ∫_a^b F(x, y, y') dx. The classical theory tacitly assumes y has higher derivatives y'', y''', .... To close this 'Lücke' (gap):
- approximate y uniformly by rational functions (Weierstrass-type approximation);
- the first variation gives ∫ (F_y η + F_{y'} η') dx = 0 for all admissible η;
- integrate by parts to get ∫ (F_y − d/dx F_{y'}) η dx = 0 (my modern restatement of the step);
- show the approximants make a certain differential expression (the Euler–Lagrange expression) vanish in the limit;
- a generalization of the divisibility lemma would then transfer smoothness and differentiability to the limit function.
No computation is carried out. It is a research plan for a regularity theorem: an extremal of a regular problem has continuous higher derivatives.

(2) Philosophical remark. Every mathematical theorem can be put positively as an existence statement of the form: to every system of numbers with property P one can assign (zuordnen) a system of numbers with property Q. The first system is the given or constant; the second is the sought, variable, arbitrary. The properties are themselves expressed by such assignments, 'and so the whole building rises'. In modern terms, every theorem is a ∀∃ statement (a Skolem-function or correspondence form), with properties defined recursively by the same kind of statement.

(3) Research suggestion on Fermat's Last Theorem. To prove x^λ + y^λ = z^λ has no nontrivial solutions, study the function field defined by y^λ + x^λ + 1 = 0 (the affine Fermat curve, dehomogenized with z = −1, up to sign). The study should be number-theoretic in Kronecker's sense: build up its prime ideals, etc. (continued on the next page). He suggests first treating the case y^2 + 1 = 0. This is a call for the arithmetic of the algebraic function field of the Fermat curve, in the spirit of Kronecker and Dedekind–Weber. No derivation is given.
